  "notes": "{一人|ひとり} uses an irregular native Japanese reading (not いちにん). It means \"one person\" and also \"alone.\"\n\nIRREGULAR COUNTING:\n- {一人|ひとり}: one person\n- {二人|ふたり}: two people\n- {三人|さんにん}: three people (regular from here)\n\nCOMMON EXPRESSIONS:\n- {一人|ひとり}で: alone, by oneself\n- {一人|ひとり}{暮|ぐ}らし: living alone\n- {一人|ひとり}{旅|たび}: solo travel\n- {一人|ひとり}{前|まえ}: one serving; fully-fledged adult\n- {一人|ひとり}っ{子|こ}: only child\n\nNOTE: {一人|ひとり}で can imply doing something independently (positive) or being lonely (context-dependent).",
